course: dictionary
lesson: 3082
title: Educate and its family of words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Educate is a verb, a doing word. It means to teach someone over a long time, especially at school, college or university. «Parents and teachers educate children» means they help children learn, step by step, over many years.
explain: Educate can also mean to give people information so they understand something better, such as about health or safety. A leaflet can educate people about road safety. The context tells you which meaning is being used: is it about years of school, or about giving information?
explain: Adding -tion often turns a doing word into a thing word. «Adding -tion often turns a doing word (verb) into a thing word (noun): inform becomes information, educate becomes education.» Education is the thing word, and it names the process of learning. Check each meaning by putting it into the sentence to see if it still makes sense.
fact: Educate is a verb: educate, educates, educated, educating.
fact: «Educate» means to teach someone over a long time, especially at school, college or university.
fact: «Educate» can also mean to give people information so they understand better, such as about health or safety.
fact: «Educated» can mean you went to a school, as in «be educated at» a school.
fact: «Education» is the thing word made from educate, and it names the process of learning.
exercise: «Parents and teachers educate children.» What does educate mean here? | To make someone angry | To stop someone from learning | *To teach someone over a long time | To buy something new
exercise: A campaign wants to educate people about recycling. What does educate mean here? | To tidy up the street | To close a shop | To teach someone at university only | *To give people information so they understand better
exercise: Adding -tion to a doing word makes a thing word, as in «educate becomes education». Which is the thing word? | educating | educated | educate | *education
test: «She was well educated and loved to read.» In this sentence, what does well educated mean? | *Had a good education and learned a lot | Had lots of friends at school | Was good at running races | Was very tired after a long day
test: «In the past, many children, especially girls, were not educated.» What does this tell you? | *Many of those children did not go to school to learn | Many of those children were top of the class | Many of those children became teachers | Many of those children loved their school
exam: Which sentence uses educate to mean giving people information? | *«The leaflet educates people about road safety.» | «The school educates children every day.» | «The teacher educated her class for three years.» | «The children were educated at a village school.»
